{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B642OI', 'answerTime': 'Jul 9, 2006', 'unixTime': 1152428400, 'question': 'Hints for Lab 35: Are there any hints available online?', 'answer': "Yes ... I solved all but 5 of the 200 levels on my own ... but was stuck on a few of them so I searched for some solutions ... here are two great websites with pictures and text solutions for all of the levels. Don't give up too soon .. but if stuck they can get you moving again. Both sites are in German, but you can figure the solutions out either using the pictures or using a website translator. Good Luck. http://home.tiscali.de/troll/cm/index.html http://www.cm-fanpage.de/board/index.php"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B642OI', 'answerTime': 'Jan 14, 2007', 'unixTime': 1168761600, 'question': 'Hints for #16: Blinker Device: Any advice?', 'answer': 'we are stuck at 16 also, wondering if there is a mistake on this one??'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Nov 21, 2007', 'unixTime': 1195632000, 'question': 'Is this compatible with Windows Vista? Is this version of MasterCook compatible with Vista?', 'answer': "FYI, I have it running on a Vista machine and have encountered the following issues (none of which are deal breakers for me) 1) Can't access Help (written in older version) 2) Import Wizard doesn't import ingredients (?? - everything else moves over just fine) 3) Web Import doesn't function"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Apr 22, 2012', 'unixTime': 1335078000, 'question': 'Master Cook compatible with Mac?', 'answer': 'Sorry. Since MC5, no Mac version. Only MC2, 3, 4 work on Mac.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Oct 23, 2011', 'unixTime': 1319353200, 'question': 'Key Gourmet for Windows by SoftKey International: Is there a program that I can purchase where I can transfer my recipes from his computer to a new program.', 'answer': 'Greetings...just in case you are the owner of a 64 bit edition of Windows 7 I have successfully imported my recipes from Key Gourmet into Living Cookbook 2011. I just did the first one in less than 3 minutes and now I know what I am doing you can get it down to a couple minutes per recipe. Beats the tweedle out of losing the recipes, or keeping an old machine around just for KG. I am going to write up a "how-to" doc for my wife to use so she can help (we have over 500 recipes in Key Gourmet). If you would like a copy just post a reply to this.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Nov 2, 2009', 'unixTime': 1257148800, 'question': 'Can you print your own cookbook with this program once you compile your favorite recipes? Can this product do this?', 'answer': "Hello, I haven't used MasterCook 9.0, but have used and use much earlier versions of MC--4.0 and 5.0--for precisely your pupose. My sister and I started out using and choosing from thier formats, but our book got so large, we are now using it in combination with Publisher so we can add additional features separate from the recipes. I'm not able to comment on the other product you mention. We started our project a little over 10 years ago. MC was the best one we found at the time. For health reasons and others, it has taken us a good while to complete our project, but we've found MC 4.0 and 5.0, even as old as they are, still great resources. Easy to use, quite a few recipe layout options, 5.0 has more complete nutrition information, places to write notes/family anecdotes at the start or finish of your recipe, flexibility with the ingredient list, in case you are using something not included there or are using another recipe as an ingredient. I imagine 9.0 is even better, more updated, though like I say, I can't speak to it directly. Hopefully someone else can help you with that. Good luck with your project. CV"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Jan 5, 2010', 'unixTime': 1262678400, 'question': 'MasterCook compatibility with Windows 7? However, I am concerned about the ability of MasterCook to run with Windows 7?', 'answer': 'We have been using Mastercook 3 running on our old XP system. Just bought a new computer with Windows 7 64 bit. Tried to port the Mastercook over but got an error message that I need a 32 bit or 64 bit version. Is anyone updating this program for Windows 7? Would buy a new copy if it was supported on Windows 7 and I could port my old recipes to it.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'May 18, 2009', 'unixTime': 1242630000, 'question': 'Making a shopping list without taking it from a recipe: Does MasterCook 9.0 do this?', 'answer': 'Yes, in the Shopping List section, it has a column entitled, Store Location (produce, frozen vegetables, canned fruit and juices, etc.)'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Jun 30, 2008', 'unixTime': 1214809200, 'question': 'macintosh: Is anyone using this on a MAC?', 'answer': "Yes, I have used it for years. I love all the features and have logged 419 of our families recipes including pictures with hopes of a cookbook someday. However, that said, it has not been Mac friendly, the updates have included the PC only. So, that leaves the Mac users still using the old version 4.0.6. Meaning, that as new cooking ingredients come to market, you have to add them in yourself. It has huge print problems. I found a workaround, saving the recipes as a PDF and then printing. Another big draw back is having to use it in 'Classic. But, I am exictied today I got an email bulletin about Mariner Software's Mac Gourmet Deluxe. I am in the process of reviewing it with high hopes. Check it out."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Oct 20, 2007', 'unixTime': 1192863600, 'question': 'Copying recipes from old version to version 9.0 Master Cook: Can I copy my own recipes entered in Master Cook Cooking Light Version 4.07 into Master Cook Version 9.0?', 'answer': "I successfully converted all my recipes from my 1995 Mac version of Master Cook Cooking Light to Master Cook Version 9.0. While all the recipes transferred, my menu's did not. Attaching a recipe to a menu in the older versions was far easier than the current, a major disappointment, but it's still the best software for capturing your own recipes and organizing meals."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Jan 29, 2008', 'unixTime': 1201593600, 'question': 'Copying/Transferring Cookbook from one computer to another: The question is, how can I transfer multiple folders which may contain multiple files (recipes)?', 'answer': 'I just did this for my wife on her new computer. I did hers on our home network, but in explorer you can copy the cookbook files to a cd on your computer and then from the cd on his computer using drag and drop or copy files function. At least it worked for me. Good luck.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B7TBNE', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424937600, 'question': 'where can I find the mastercook 9 patch', 'answer': "You can't. That old version is no longer supported. It will NOT run correctly on the latest versions of Windows either. It fact it usually makes people's recipes completely disappear from the program as explained here: http://support.mastercook.com/entries/22055813-Recipes-Disappear-from-Cookbook The current version of MasterCook is designed and compatible with the latest versions of Windows. It can use all of your files from version 9 too as explained here: http://support.mastercook.com/entries/22017556-Transferring-Recipes-and-Files-from-a-Previous-Version"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B8K7SG', 'answerTime': 'May 20, 2006', 'unixTime': 1148108400, 'question': 'Do you have to buy new product every year? I am new at using this and I was wondering if you have to buy the new version of the software every year or if there are updates to add to a past version that you can use?', 'answer': 'You have to buy the new version each year.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B8K7SG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405407600, 'question': 'Why would anyone buy the old version of this program. Particularly at FULL price?', 'answer': 'Wow, what a question.. but the answer (in my world) to your question - is because (for many devastating, personal reasons) I needed to file "back taxes"... I had used Turbo Tax 2004 - & turbo tax provided us with that 2005 resource (in 2012). It worked great - and honestly - getting caught up - that was worth 10x the "full price"... just sayin\'. so THAT is why.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000B8K7SG', 'answerTime': 'Jun 10, 2013', 'unixTime': 1370847600, 'question': 'is this a windows version?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Apparently it contains both the Mac and Windows versions in the same box. This box though contains the 2005 Federal tax return forms. Is that what you want? If so, the Windows version is certified to run on Windows XP. If the computer that you plan to run it on has a more modern version of Windows (like Vista, 7 or 8), it may not run.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000B8K7SG', 'answerTime': 'Apr 5, 2006', 'unixTime': 1144220400, 'question': 'Why buy this? Wondering why I should buy this software for $35 while I can do my tax and submit it online for only $10 (also using TurboTax)???', 'answer': "Unless you have a very simple return, I personally do not like the online systems. I like the ability to have my data file right on my local disk. You are also not at the mercy of either Intuit's server (which may be busy, or having problems), or your internat connection (ISP, etc)."}
