{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Apr 21, 2007', 'unixTime': 1177138800, 'question': 'bootcamp or parallels? Is the bootcamp performance better?', 'answer': 'Absolutely better. Boot Camp uses both processor cores and all your RAM, whereas Parallels cannot.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Sep 29, 2007', 'unixTime': 1191049200, 'question': 'Beware of Rebate offers from Nova Development (owner of Parallel Desktop) Guess what?', 'answer': 'I had the same problem. After purchasing Parallels 2.5 in March 2007 from Amazon and carefully sending in all the requisite materials no rebate appeared in my mailbox. A call to Nova informed me that they had indeed received my materials, but one item was "missing" (they claim the UPC from the retail box) and thus no rebate. Parallels is a decent application, but I don\'t believe that Nova is honestly fulfilling their rebate offers. Caveat emptor!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Jul 29, 2007', 'unixTime': 1185692400, 'question': 'Virus protection on Mac w/Windows? If it really is "necessary" and I opt NOT to run the protection, only Windows (&amp; any programs/files associated) would be affected -- not the Mac OS -- right?', 'answer': 'I would like to know how you ran Parallels on a G4 machine. The box says it is only compatible with Intel-based Macs. Parallels has a snapshot feature. If you get infected with a virus, you can always revert back to the last snapshot before the infection. Hint: take a snapshot after setting up Windows without a network connection, and after configuring it the way you like it. Then go onto the internet. A full install of the Parallels toolset opens up access to the Mac disc drives. Although a Windows virus would not infect the Mac environment via Parallels, it is possible that using an infected Windows Word to edit a Word doc on the Mac would definitely lead to a Macro virus infection of .doc files on the Mac. Sharing such a file with your correspondents would send them the virus, and they would then be infected. Also, Trojan Horses can execute code that can have devastating effects, especially if targetting the Mac. I am not aware of any of this type, but there is always a first time...'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Dec 7, 2007', 'unixTime': 1197014400, 'question': 'Will Parallels 3.0 Work with Leopard (Max OS X 10.5) If you buy this product, will you have to turn around and buy another copy that works with Leopard a few weeks from now?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Get Parallel 3.0 build 5582, which was release on 12/5/07. Earlier builds were not fully compatible with Leopard. I purchased an earlier build of 3.0 and parallel would not even start w/ Leopard. This problem has been reported on the web by other users. I upgraded to 5582, and it works beautifully. MCHP'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Jun 21, 2007', 'unixTime': 1182409200, 'question': 'Is the version Amazon.com selling 3.0? Does anyone know if the version Amazon is shipping is 3.0?', 'answer': 'nope its the release before'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Mar 27, 2007', 'unixTime': 1174978800, 'question': 'can this work with apple mac book pro(new),using windows vista ultimate, and microsoft office 2007 as log as Parellels desktop for mac is installed first, the other software? I am new to apple products. thank you , Renee: IS it possible for parallels...', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I think the answer is yes. I'm running vista without any problems. Ofice should also work. you might want to [post your question in the mac support forums: http://discussions.apple.com/index.jspa or the parallels forums: http://forum.parallels.com/ where you will get a more expert answer"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Nov 2, 2007', 'unixTime': 1193986800, 'question': 'Parallels or VMWare Fusion? So, what would you go with?', 'answer': 'E. Marcucci, I was had a similar situation where I wanted to run real Windows applications. I originally purchased Parallels because I really like the graphic implementation however after 2 unexpected crashes I ended up returning it. The biggest problem I had with Parallels is they have horrid support. Honestly, the support is what really did me in. When I posted to the Parallels I never got a straight answer from a Parallel employee. I ended up sending a private message to one I found and he was helpful but it was a little late. Additionally, e-mail support was terrible and could never get a straight answer about how to return the product. I went with VMWare and have been happy. I was able to successfully use VMWare converter that converted my old Thinkpad laptop to a VMWare image and dump it on the Mac. It work with it every day. My company only support Windows operating system and never had any problem with the converted image. This is one opinion but the choice is yours. Parallels lost a customer with their lack of product support. Thanks'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Jun 8, 2007', 'unixTime': 1181286000, 'question': 'Non-Intel MAC (Power PC G5) Will parallels or Boot Camp install on a G5 MAC?', 'answer': 'no. Only Intel-based macs. my older mac, a G4 (PPC) gets an error during install saying you must have an intel processor. Sorry'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Mar 8, 2008', 'unixTime': 1204963200, 'question': 'Parallels vs VMware to run Adobe CS3: Does anyone have experience of running pc based CS3 software (especially Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, Flash and also Corel Painter) with Parallels or VMware Fusion?', 'answer': "Don't bother. Highly specialized apps (like those in CS3) you want to run natively. And the mac version is very good. Call Adobe; they DO offer a cross-platform upgrade (there is cost, but it's significantly less than buying a new product). You'll be much happier in the long run, and save time running them natively."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000GHIV2Q', 'answerTime': 'Jun 21, 2007', 'unixTime': 1182409200, 'question': 'PC games on IMac: I desperatly want to run pc games on my IMac, Would boot came or parallels be better?', 'answer': 'the version on amazon of parallels doesnt even support games in windows, so I would recommend bootcamp. The new version of parallels (3.0) does support games, but you will experience much better gaming performance if you run windows from bootcamp'}
