{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001AMHWP8', 'answerTime': 'Mar 29, 2011', 'unixTime': 1301382000, 'question': 'Can I upgrade from 10.4? Can I upgrade my laptop which currently has os 10.4.11?', 'answer': "I have an imac core2duo from late 2006/early 2007 that was running Tiger 10.4.11. I ordered this under $30 update disc to upgrade to snow leopard after being told at the local Apple store that it positively would not work and I needed the expensive box set instead. Well, so much for their geniuses-it worked just fine. 45 minutes to go to 10.6.3 then another 30 to download all of the newest updates and it's running perfectly. I did order a 2 gig memory module to take me to 3 gig total, the most my machine can recognize. This is definitely worth a try if you are thinking of upgrading. I also did a complete backup of my hard drive first. It would also be handy to have an empty external drive on hand (320-500 gigs are available cheap) to set up for time machine backups. Good luck-go for it!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001AMHWP8', 'answerTime': 'Aug 1, 2012', 'unixTime': 1343804400, 'question': 'At these prices ??? Why would anyone consider buying the Snow Leopard upgrade from Amazon or any of these vendors when Apple is selling it for $29?', 'answer': "Hey - I was going to tell everyone - DO NOT BUY LEOPARD FOR MORE THAN 29.00. Apple if you call them will sell you a copy of the Snow Leopard for 29.00. I just bought mine today due to some bought software that wouldn't work with 10.5.8"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001AMHWP8', 'answerTime': 'Jun 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1401692400, 'question': 'I have Mac 10.4.11. Will this upgrade work for my computer?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It did upgrade some of my functions but it also has caused a bunch of problems. I would suggest that you take your MAC to an expert instead of doing it yourself, unless you are very good at computers.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001AMHWP8', 'answerTime': 'May 4, 2011', 'unixTime': 1304492400, 'question': 'Use to start over? Is this disc strictly an "upgrade" or can I use it to install the OS on an empty drive?', 'answer': "You can install it without need for Tiger, Leopard, etc. I think that legally, you should have Tiger or Leopard to install Snow Leopard, but I wouldn't recommend upgrading over an existing system. It's OK if you don't have iLife or iWork, as when I bought my Mac over three years ago (had Tiger) that didn't have serial number for iWork, and so I'd assume that any 'bonus' software like that you'd have to pay for anyway. Hope this helps. Don't use Time Machine--just copy all your important documents and whatever else to the other drive manually. That's what I do. I don't trust backup programs (many people don't--I've read some horror stories in the past on data loss)."}
