{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003B32B2I', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2012', 'unixTime': 1330243200, 'question': 'Is $259 a good price for Photoshop CS5 ? Is this option even possible with PS?', 'answer': '@ A. doeling The main difference between Elements and CS5 (in regard to photos) is that Elements is simpler program for people with simpler needs (i.e. the difference between a hobbyist and a professional). Elements has more buttons that do things is a more "automatic" fashion, while CS5 gets more in-depth with the same functions so that a person can fine tune what they need done. In general, if Elements has a tool or function, CS5 has the same tool or function but does it a lot better. But in some cases CS5 has tools that Elements just plain doesn\'t have, such as layer masks and compositing capabilities (to name a few). If you are looking for a program built more toward hobbyists and scrapbookers, Elements is the way to go. But if you\'re looking for a professional tool with more advanced capabilities, CS5 is well worth the upgrade.'}
