{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047B0U1C', 'answerTime': 'Aug 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1377154800, 'question': 'I have windows8 will linux work ok on the laptop?', 'answer': 'It should work, but there can always be driver issues. Some hardware components are proprietary and may not have full function. But its easy to test. Just go to the ubuntu website download it and run it live off a usb. This way you wont make any permanent changes to your laptop. I normally dont install any linux software on my computer which are all windows and I run linux of a micro usb dongle. so first download ubuntu. Then go to http://www.pendriveapps.com/uui-universal-usb-installer/ and download uui - universal installer Run uui and burn ubuntu onto a usb. Once you burned it onto a usb, you can boot from usb. But make sure your bios is set to let you boot from usb before booting from hard drive. If your bios is set to boot from usb before harddrive your set. Restart your computer and let the linux load from usb. Once loaded you can play around with it and see if anything does not work or if everythingn is fine. I use a micro dongle, with linux install on it. And because its so small I usually dont remove it unless I need to use windows. Because its running from my usb and saves information on the usb it doesnt make any permanent changes to my laptop, but presently i am running windows 7. By the way, go to ubuntu.com to download ubuntu. Dont buy it online since ubuntu is free from the creators.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047B0U1C', 'answerTime': 'Oct 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413874800, 'question': 'what size usb drive is required to burn ubuntu to the drive?', 'answer': '4gb or bigger USB drive will suffice nicely. USB drives are cheap so bigger is better!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047B0U1C', 'answerTime': 'Sep 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1409900400, 'question': 'will linux load on a new hard drive, the old hard drive has been removed', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It should.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047B0U1C', 'answerTime': 'Jul 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405148400, 'question': 'Have old desktop running XP - LinuxLive overwrite not working - if I reformat (to kill XP), will disks load OK?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'In my opinion you should treat Linux as a completely new O.S. Do a complete backup of your XP: files, format your HDD, and boot from your DVD I think that you will be running Ubuntu. It is the better of the bunch. Make sure that it is 14.04, not 13.x.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047B0U1C', 'answerTime': 'Jun 26, 2013', 'unixTime': 1372230000, 'question': "Will Linux run if vista has problems booting? I've tried to restore/repair vista, failed. Will this run on crappy lappy?", 'answer': "Yes. That's exactly why I got it. I tried to restore my copy of Vista as well, and it worked ok at first but then went right back to being fussy again. The Linux actually deletes Vista from the drive completely and replaces it with Linux. Stylistically, it looks a little different than Windows, but not much. If you're used to the way Windows looks, it should only take 10 or 15 minutes at most to figure out where to find things in Linux. I think I had to find an e-mail program to use because it doesn't come with one. Thunderbird works well and is easy to figure out."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047UEXP2', 'answerTime': 'Jan 17, 2011', 'unixTime': 1295251200, 'question': 'Premium price increase: Why did premium suddenly go from $32 to $57', 'answer': "Because Amazon's prices like any retailer are not written in stone. You see a nice deal, get it when you see it."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047UEXP2', 'answerTime': 'Jan 22, 2011', 'unixTime': 1295683200, 'question': 'Software vs. online filing?', 'answer': 'I have always used Block (formerly Taxcut). It has always cost less to buy (price wise) and does the job with no hidden klinkers.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047UEXP2', 'answerTime': 'Jan 12, 2011', 'unixTime': 1294819200, 'question': 'How much does a state e-file cost? Can someone tell me how much?', 'answer': "Well, it depends on the revision you use. If you by the software to install on your computer it's $19.95, if you use the internet verison it is what the chart says... $35"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047UEXP2', 'answerTime': 'Feb 25, 2011', 'unixTime': 1298620800, 'question': "Can't import data from Charles Schwab: Is this correct?", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "That's correct; you cannot import info directly from Schwab into the H&R Block software. I've used Tax Cut for years (I'm a Schwab customer), and I copy the info I need from Schwab into an Excel file, and then use a third party macro (from InvestoTeK, called ConvertExcel2TXF, $5.00) to make the conversion of the info from Schwab into the needed format (.TXF file) which can be imported. I discovered this last year when doing our 2009 taxes and it works great. You just pay for the macro and download it online. (I have no connection to InvestoTek except as a satisfied customer). FYI if you are a Schwab customer you can buy TurboTax through them for a discount. I just prefer the H&R Block software to TT."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047UEXP2', 'answerTime': 'Jan 23, 2011', 'unixTime': 1295769600, 'question': "Is H&amp;R Block Q&amp;A only or can you go to forms and start? Where I'm going with this is I want to do a return on my own in forms mode and then do one with the interview method and see what gives the best result.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'In my experience using H&R B for the last several years, you can manually complete any form. You may, however, run into some issues when the program carries entries from one form to another. You do have the option to override entries, so the manual process should be workable, if not totally painless.'}
