{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Q0PSMK', 'answerTime': 'Feb 22, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424592000, 'question': 'I want to upgrade a Laptop currently with 32 bit version of Windows VISTA to Windows 7. What do I buy?', 'answer': 'First things first. Make sure you have all of the Windows 7 drivers for your laptop motherboard. Also make sure it meets the minimum requirements for the OS. Any of the Windows 7 OS should work for you, except Enterprise of course. Hope this helps, good luck.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Q0PSMK', 'answerTime': 'Jan 16, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389859200, 'question': 'what is sp1? i have a 5 year old computer with windows xp.would my driver handle this?', 'answer': 'sp1 means Service Pak 1 it is a consolidation of numerous patches,security updates and other updates to Windows 7. The minimum hardware requirements are: 1 gigahertz (GHz) or faster 32-bit (x86) or 64-bit (x64) processor 1 gigabyte (GB) RAM (32-bit) or 2 GB RAM (64-bit) 16 GB available hard disk space (32-bit) or 20 GB (64-bit) DirectX 9 graphics device with WDDM 1.0 or higher driver'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004Q0PSMK', 'answerTime': 'Nov 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385107200, 'question': 'Does this Win7 come with a Product Activation Key? I noticed in some of the comments that they had problems in finding a key or in registering.', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'This is a fully functioning developer version factory sealed by Microsoft. The product key is included. I had no problems installing or registering, but this is NOT the retail version so you will not have any technical support available.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Q0PSMK', 'answerTime': 'Feb 7, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423296000, 'question': 'i want to know, if I can upgrade from XP to 7, with this? Another question, is 1 gig ram enough to run 7?', 'answer': '2 GBs is the recommended minimum. I upgraded to Win 7 from XP, but I had 4 GBs memory. Works fine.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Q0T10A', 'answerTime': 'Sep 28, 2013', 'unixTime': 1380351600, 'question': 'Xp to 7', 'answer': 'Hi David I can give you my experiance. I own a 3 year old Dell 410 desk top PC. It needed an upgrade to keep up with newer software. Step 1 More memory Max for this model. 2 New hard drive, went with the new Solid Sate Digital Drive 60 Gigabyte. To make this happen needed to purchace Windows 7 Home Premium SP1 32bit (OEM) System Builder DVD 1 Pack. OS does have support for SSD drives, with other experienced tweaks these were the results. Windows XP to 7 with the upgrades mentioned. The entire prosses took me about 3 hours. 2 hours to install 7 and all my other software. Has run flawless out of the box. computer start up went from 5 to 7 minutes to 92 secounds. from user screen log on it was 3.5 minutes now 23 seconds. All made possible by the product you want to purchase. I hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Q0T10A', 'answerTime': 'Dec 28, 2012', 'unixTime': 1356681600, 'question': 'where is the windows office with WORD program?', 'answer': 'Microsoft\'s Office with "Word" has never been included with Windows 7 Home Premium. It is purchased individually as a separate product. Back 2 or 3 years ago MS office was included in most new laptop and PC purchases but this has also stopped being true. What most new laptops and PC\'s offer is Microsoft Office starter. This is a reduced functionality Word and Excel with advertising. Pretty much useless. There is "Open Office" which is free, fully functional and compatible to Microsoft office. Just Google around for it, you\'ll find it. There is also Google business apps which is finally beginning to cut into Microsoft\'s market share, however... it is cheap, but not free. Good Luck!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004Q0T10A', 'answerTime': 'Jul 27, 2013', 'unixTime': 1374908400, 'question': 'can i install windows 7 premium (oem)sys.builder dvd 1pack on more than one machine?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'No you cannot. You must have one unique (paid for) Product Key for each machine you want to install Windows 7 on. The System Builder DVD gives you one product key good for one machine only. You can buy additional licenses, however. I don\'t know the details of how to use or take advantage of Microsoft\'s "Volume Licensing". I don\'t install Microsoft Operating Systems in large enough volume to justify the cost of a volume license. However the idea is you get one product key per machine -- something like that. What you can be sure of is that if you install a Microsoft Operating System from a DVD, be prepared to type in a unique, paid-for product key for each machine you install on. You can browse http://www.microsoft.com/oem to get more information about their System Builder program, including the licensing.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Q0T10A', 'answerTime': 'Dec 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387267200, 'question': 'What is the difference between the OEM version and the Full Upgrade version other than the price? I am planning to upgrade from an old XP system.', 'answer': 'You will be far better off doing a fresh install rather than an upgrade. Definitely worth the price difference.'}
