{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005N2CS00', 'answerTime': 'Jul 8, 2013', 'unixTime': 1373266800, 'question': 'I have Family Tree Maker 2005 on my hard drive. I cannot find my original disk. What do I need to be able to transfer my info to a new computer?', 'answer': "If you have done research and have saved information you found from a site like Ancestry.com, there should be a file or files with your family name with the file extension '.FTM'. For instance, Jones.FTM. This is found, in Windows, under: My Documents/Family Tree Maker. You can then import or transfer that file to you new computer. If you are asking if you can install Family Tree Maker on a new computer without the original install CDs, then I think you will have to buy a new copy of FTM. Sorry."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005N2CS00', 'answerTime': 'Mar 27, 2013', 'unixTime': 1364367600, 'question': 'I have older version of Family Tree Maker -- version 10 for XP. I recently bought new laptop running Windows 8. Do I need to buy newer version?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Version 10 should run fine on Win8. I have a Win8 box that I recently installed a lot of XP vintage software on and have had no issues. I was using 10 but switched to Essentials 2012 because of issues with merging web info with my tree. Ancestry support told me what I was seeing was a known issue and would likely not be corrected. They suggested I upgrade because the latest version doesn't have the problem and so far it has been fine. I'm still running Essentials 2012 on my XP box because that is where my files are but I'll eventually reinstall and move everything to the Win8 machine."}
