{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'Jan 27, 2013', 'unixTime': 1359273600, 'question': "What are the options to 'upload' all my written recipes with your software? This is a big part of what I need.", 'answer': "You can put your own recipes in this software. I did a cookbook for my children a few years ago and also put most of the recipes from it onto my Cook'n. you can also make your own cookbook from the software but at the time i found it easier to use MS Publisher. I need to do another printing because i have gotten many more requests for it, so may try it this time from Cook'n and see if it's easier with version 10. But it is a good place to organize all of your own family recipes."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'May 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1369206000, 'question': 'Are my Cookbook recipes that i input located on your server or on my computer data base/? I lost access to the internet on my desktop.', 'answer': 'Your recipe database is located on your computer.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'Mar 2, 2013', 'unixTime': 1362211200, 'question': 'A review said you had to enter Brand Names on ingredients - Is that correct?', 'answer': 'No, this is not correct. The software gives you the option of entering specific brand information when entering a recipe but it is not required. If you add new ingredients to the ingredient list, you also have the option of entering the corresponding Brand information but it is not required. FYI...the Brand information is used by the software to compute nutrition facts, estimate grocery shopping costs, and so forth. One of the neat things about Cook\'n is that you can actually switch from one brand to another and get a different nutritional result. This makes it possible for you to "healthify" a recipe by simply changing brands. It\'s pretty powerful that way.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'Jan 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388995200, 'question': 'version 10 vs. version 11', 'answer': "Version 10 I think It was such a waste of space I uninstalled it. It seems like - since I registered with them online - I got a product offering email from them about 'new features'. So there's probably a newer version. I have the Food Network App on my iPhone now and it has everything my wife and I need for cooking for parties with friends, holidays, etc. Hope that helps. Mountainwestman"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'Jun 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404025200, 'question': "I would like to know more about the scanning feature. Does it have it's own O.C.R. program included?", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The way I used it is this..scan your recipe. Access Cook\'n. Hit the scan feature. Choose the recipe that you scanned from my scans. When the recipe shows up on the left, you highlight the item...i.e. "name", the in the box that has the functions, click name and it show up on the line for name. Do the same for description, ingredients and directions. Very easy. I love this feature. This option is available with Cook\'n 11.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'Dec 14, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387008000, 'question': 'does it work with windows 8', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Absolutely. I asked the company that question myself before buying it. Yes, it works well with Windows 8. You will love it.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0068M5ZCM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 11, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392105600, 'question': 'Is it easy to use', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes it is. There is a video on the Cook\'n website that you can watch to see how to use it. I love it because: I can import recipes from other sites. I can add my own in a "cookbook" that I create in the program into which I can add my own recipes. You will receive a monthly newsletter with recipes and other information. You can easily click on any of those recipes and then import those into Cook\'n\'s recipe book.'}
