{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008MR38ZM', 'answerTime': 'May 29, 2013', 'unixTime': 1369810800, 'question': 'Once I print out the documents and have them notarized, does that make them "legal", or do I need to file them with my county courthouse?', 'answer': "You need to get copies NOTARIZED by a Notary Public. You need to sign copies, too. I do not know what you have to do beyond that. Why don't you call your local courthouse?"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008MR38ZM', 'answerTime': 'Nov 26, 2012', 'unixTime': 1353916800, 'question': 'is the information you put in only on your computer and kept private--I do not want the information going to any site or place other than my pc', 'answer': 'Yes, all of the information you enter into the program resides and stays on your computer -- it is accessible only from your PC. Additionally, you have the option to password protect the program, so that you have to know the password to get to your documents. Hope that helps! -- Betsy Simmons Hannibal, Nolo editor of Quicken WillMaker Plus'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B008MR38ZM', 'answerTime': 'Nov 26, 2012', 'unixTime': 1353916800, 'question': 'Will it read the .lgl files from Quicken Family Lawyer, V. 6.0b ?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'No, the files created by Quicken Family Lawyer are incompatible with Quicken WillMaker Plus. However, Quicken WillMaker Plus 2013 is compatible with all prior versions of Quicken WillMaker, as well as Quicken Lawyer Personal and Quicken Lawyer Wills (from 2002 and 2003). For more about compatibility, see the Quicken WillMaker FAQ at http://www.nolo.com/technical-support/quicken-willmaker.html#Version . Hope that helps! -- Betsy Simmons Hannibal, Nolo editor of Quicken WillMaker Plus'}
