{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00B1TEIRU', 'answerTime': 'Feb 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391414400, 'question': 'Can you edit docs created with 365 on a computer that has a reg install of Office? As in my son creates a presentation at home but needs to update it', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes. if the document is stores in the cloud just download it to your computer and treat it like any other document. The only caveat is that 365 is the latest version of Word and you might have to save the document as a previous version.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00B1TEIRU', 'answerTime': 'Mar 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1363071600, 'question': 'After first computer, how do you load to subsequent devices?', 'answer': 'From the computer you want to install it on go to office.microsoft.com Next step is to sign in. Once signed in, click on the "My Account" shortcut (or click on your name on top right corner and an option to access account pops up). You\'ll see your product, how many installs you have left and a green button that will install 365 to your computer.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00B1TEIRU', 'answerTime': 'Apr 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397458800, 'question': 'I know it\'s a yearly subscription, but what if I opt out next year? Can I still access my files on a computer that has the "real" Office installed?', 'answer': 'This is one of those classic "it depends" answers. Office 365 uses the newest file format for office . If your "real" office installation (office 2013) can access that new format, the answer is "yes." If you opt out you\'ll also not lose your ability to view those files, just the ability to edit/modify the files. You also can maintain backwards compatibility by doing a "save as" and choosing one of the older file formats. If you take that last option, you do lose some of the formatting options that are available in 0ffice 2013/office 365 and apps will indicate that you are using "compatibility mode."'}
