{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00COJ3BE2', 'answerTime': 'Nov 25, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416902400, 'question': 'Do you need to connect a mic to the computer for it to evaluate you? If so, what kind of mic?', 'answer': "I have a headset with mic, but any handheld mic that fits your computer's input would work. I have not tried it with my laptop's built in mic. It would probably work but I don't know if it would have the same quality as a plug in."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00COJ3BE2', 'answerTime': 'Dec 8, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386489600, 'question': 'I would like to understand the voice input device requirements. Do we need to purchase a USB microphone, or just use the built in microphone.', 'answer': 'Build in microphone is OK, but you should use Earphones... otherwise program cannot distinguish between you and itself. By the way, the program is great fun to use. I use it every day and I improved a lot... Georg in Germany.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00COJ3BE2', 'answerTime': 'Nov 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385366400, 'question': 'Is this similar to "Singing for Dummies" CD? If not, how is it different?', 'answer': "Hi David, Singing for Dummies and eMedia Singing Method draw from the same body of instructional content, but Singing for Dummies contains about half the lessons present in eMedia Singing Method. eMedia Singing Method also includes the Performance Evaluation feature, which will listen to you sing the whole song, give you specific feedback on mistakes, and give you an overall score. This feature is not present in Singing for Dummies. Both Singing for Dummies and eMedia Singing Method include the Interactive Feedback feature, which tells you whether you're in tune, too high or too low in pitch. If you have any questions about eMedia Music software, please contact us at http://www.emediamusic.com/contact/contact_cs.html. Thanks, Sam from eMedia Music Customer Service"}
