{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00H09BB16', 'answerTime': 'Feb 6, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423209600, 'question': 'Is this eligible for free windows 10 upgrade?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "As of today (2-6-15) it is. But Microsoft has been changing their upgrade path plans as they get closer to release. So by time 10 is out, they may have changed the rules on what will be excepted. As of now it's only Windows 7 and 8."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00H09BB16', 'answerTime': 'Oct 30, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414652400, 'question': 'Will this wipe my PC clean and make it like it came from the factory?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Yes, if that is what you want it to do. And, no if you do not. You have the chose to, format your HDD, hard disk drive, re-install saving your documents, pictures and downloaded files, provided that you currently have Windows 7 installed on your PC. Or, repair the current OS provided it is Win 7 Home Premium. Also, be aware of what bit OS you have if you are upgrading. i.e. 64 bit or 32 bit. You can not down-grade, go from, say Win 8 to Win 7. You must select to format the HDD if your are going from a 32 bit OS to a 64 bit. It is my opinion that Windows 7 Professional 64 bit Operation System is as good as it gets. Win 7 Home Premium 64 bit is sweet, too.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00H09BB16', 'answerTime': 'Aug 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1406876400, 'question': "ive heard that if i use this it'll be bound to the motherboard does that mean i wont be able to upgrade to a different OS later?", 'answer': "What being bound to the motherboard means that you won't be able to activate it on another PC. If you change the motherboard on your present PC you will have to activate by phone. There will be no problem to upgrade to say windows 8. But then you will have the same issue of that OS bound to the motherboard. I have done this many times with upgrades and PC repairs."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00H09BB16', 'answerTime': 'Mar 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1394092800, 'question': 'where is the product key?', 'answer': 'If you open the white card-stock envelope, you will find your Disc within a blue card-stock envelope. On the front of that blue envelope is a sticker... it has a barcode on the top and right sides of it, and a QR code (square checked UPC type code). To the left of the QR code is your key, along with a tiny Skeleton key symbol. The text is very small, and I had to actually break out my magnifying reading glasses that I use for detail work to read it.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00H09BOXQ', 'answerTime': 'Jul 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404198000, 'question': 'Is this Microsoft branded? Last time I purchased this, I received a disc from Dell and a License Key clearly peeled off of an old system.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Depends. Amazon is now STUPIDLY listing things under the same titles even though they ship from different sources. Ships from Amazon is likely to be legit. Ships from ANY MARKETPLACE SOURCE IS LIKELY TO BE COUNTERFEIT, RESOLD, COA (not valid), etc. because AMAZON DOES NOT CARE TO POLICE THEIR OWN MARKETPLACE.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00H09BOXQ', 'answerTime': 'May 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1401174000, 'question': 'If I install this on one hard drive, can I do a clean install of it later on? On a different hard drive for the same computer?', 'answer': "Yes, I'm sure you can. As I understand it, Microsoft maintains a record of your Windows software associated with your computer, not your hard drive. Your clean install at a later time will require deleting the original install if both drives are present at the time. In a worse case scenario, you might have to call Microsoft customer support if you have a glitch; just explain to them what you are trying to do and they will get you through it."}
